## Tool-use efficiency

This repo has <large generated files / long test suites (`<test cmd>` ≈ <N> min) / a `git status`
that lists 100+ untracked files>. Burn-rate adds up fast.

- **Read narrow ranges, not whole files.** `grep -n` to locate the symbol, then `Read` with
  `offset` + `limit`.
- **Never `git stash` to baseline-check lint/typecheck** — the stash output dumps the full repo
  file list. Use `git diff --name-only`, `git show HEAD:<path>`, or inspect the one file you changed.
- **Don't chase incidental build-artifact churn.** Build/test/check regenerate files under
  `<dist/build dir>`; that churn is expected and is not your change. Don't investigate, restore,
  or report it. Only react to source you actually edited. Don't invent "tidy the tree" tasks.
- **Pipe long output through `grep` at the source**: `<test cmd> 2>&1 | grep -E '<failure pattern>'`.
  Don't redirect to a file and re-read it.
- **Batch related greps into one call** (`grep -nE 'a|b|c'`) instead of three searches.
- **Don't read more memory/plan/debug files than you need.** Indexes are auto-loaded; the linked
  files are not. Open a linked file only if its one-line description is load-bearing.
- **Run targeted tests by name before a full package run** (`<test-name-filter syntax>`). A
  multi-minute sweep is rarely the right first probe.
- **Don't read large generated references whole** (`<generated reference path>`): `grep -n` for the
  section, then `Read` a tight window. Quote the real source file for load-bearing claims.
